# Authoring Core — {{ names.knowledge }} (shared by {{ names.add_project }} / {{ names.add_domain }} / {{ names.sync_local }} / {{ names.verify }})

The process and conventions for writing and maintaining the **{{ names.knowledge }}** knowledge base. The skills ({{ names.add_project }}, {{ names.add_domain }}, {{ names.sync_local }}, {{ names.verify }}) all point here as the authority: a skill's SKILL.md / CHECKLIST.md is the actionable layer, this file holds the full rationale, where to look, and the pitfalls. Rules specific to {{ identity.display_name }} live in `{{ names.local_rules }}.md`, whose sections mirror this file's numbering (§5, §5b, §6).

Target knowledge base: `{{ derived.knowledge_root }}` (adjust to where you cloned it): `references/project/`, `references/domain/`{{#if has.observability_file}}, `references/observability.md`{{/if}}{{#if has.shared_catalogs}}, `references/platform-basics.md`{{/if}} and the reader `SKILL.md`.

---

## 0. What this knowledge base is for (read first; it decides everything else)

**The core idea (the parent of every other rule)**: the value of this knowledge base is that it is *trustworthy*, not that it is *complete*. One stale fact gets quoted by an AI as authoritative, and a single wrong page lowers trust in the whole base — wrong information is worse than none. So: **trust > coverage**. Whatever can be derived from code is **not copied** (the reader goes into the repo and scans it); only what code cannot show is recorded (flows, cross-project relationships, gotchas, the *why*), and every fact carries an anchor that is cheap to re-verify (§9 grep symbol / `(unverified)` / `noncode refs:`). What decides trust is not "pick facts that never change" but "write only facts where one sweep tells you whether they changed": authoring makes a page correct *now*; the routine audit of `{{ names.sync_local }}` keeps it correct *over time*. Every sourcing standard and hard rule below expands this idea.

- **Use**: background context loaded while developing — not Q&A, not a tutorial. **Two audiences, two doc types**: a **domain doc** serves readers who only read business knowledge and may not touch code (they may have installed only this knowledge base: no repos, no live tools); a **project doc** serves engineers and AIs about to work on that project, who will clone the repo. Write domain docs so the first group can follow them on their own; write project docs assuming the second group has the repo, and point back to code.
- **Evolution rule**: when extending the base for an audience, add at the low layer (so a standalone reader can use it); never subtract at the high layer (do not dilute project-doc precision for someone's convenience). Standalone readers cannot fact-check, so trust costs more for them: trust > coverage is tightest in domain docs.
- **Tone**: describe the current state; do not write instructions.
  - Good: "Cross-cutting cache and retry run through decorators plus a resilience pipeline."
  - Bad: "To change caching start here, don't touch the service."
- **Style: a doc is a doc** — it states facts; it is not a conversation and not an after-the-fact annotation.
  - **No first or second person** (I / we / you); use impersonal statements. Good: "Requires mobile mode; desktop user agents are redirected." Bad: "You must use mobile mode or you'll be redirected."
  - **No conversational or annotation tone**: "the user said…", "basically", "actually", "there's also…", emphasis glyphs. State the fact as a definition.
  - **Be concise**: cut filler, split long parentheses, one fact per sentence; stay understandable and keep the necessary facts. Large detail points to source.
  - **Sources disagree → converge to the verified value; do not list stale variants side by side.** When sources give different values, verify the current truth and write only that. Listing every variant ("A says X, B says Y, C says Z") is noise and gives a stale value the same weight as the true one. A stale source gets at most one guard sentence against regression. This is trust > coverage at the wording level: writing a wrong variant dilutes the signal.
  - **Markers = only for exceptions; the default is unmarked.** Every statement in a doc is **code-verified** (that is this kit's contract), so **unmarked = verified**; **never write a bare `(verified)`** (zero information). **No emoji or warning glyphs** (the main reader is an AI reading every word; sirens add nothing and dilute the signal). Put the counter-intuitive fact into the sentence itself and spell out the surprise. Three text markers carry information prose cannot:
    - `(unverified — <missing repo or unreachable tool>)` — checkable by code or a live tool but not checked this time (**our** repo is not local but is cloneable, or the live tool that would check it was unreachable); a greppable to-do; see §6.
    - `(human: <{{ git.user_label }}>, <YYYY-MM-DD>)` — an important fact **code cannot show** (business why, ops practice, historical decision, who to ask); see §6 human-sourced.
    - `(external: <team>)` — authority lives in **another team's** code or settings, which we neither track nor can query, so it is never audited automatically; re-verify by asking that team or using their tool. `<team>` is an owner code ({{#if has.owners_external}}currently {{ lists.owners_external }}{{else}}none are configured as external yet{{/if}}); see §6 external-owned.
  - Parentheses that carry a **source** are provenance, not markers; keep them: `(config says X, but the value reaching the log system is Y)`, `(snapshot YYYY-MM-DD, source …)`, `(note: not containerized)`. They record *where the trap is / which unguessable source*, not the redundant word "verified". **Record only what an auditor could not guess**: a name-mismatch gotcha, a non-obvious site name, a business date → record; **obvious sources are not recorded** (a dependency manifest, a build file, a deploy config — the auditor looks there anyway; recording it is redundant, the same as a bare `(verified)`).
- **Structural skeleton diagram — flow / pipeline / state-machine docs only.** A doc describing a flow with order or branches across steps (a domain flow, a cross-service pipeline, a state machine) may carry **one** structure-level mermaid skeleton; a glossary, a flat rule set or a single-step fact **does not draw one** (it only costs tokens and maintenance). Whether to draw is decided by the **nature** of the doc, not by whether a contributor attached a picture. The drift defense is that *the picture and the prose encode different granularities*, and one fact lives in one place:
  - **The skeleton draws structure only** (stage order, decision branches as boxes and arrows) — it **does not write** procedure / method / toggle / status-value identifiers; those live only in prose. Changing a detail does not touch the picture; only a structural change does (rare and visible). **Exception: when the doc's subject is itself an ordered decision or fallback** (a resolution priority, say), drawing the branches **in order** as a decision tree is *structure*, not "priority detail" — but each branch's identifier (which cookie, which header, which toggle) still stays in prose.
  - **Draw at the doc's own altitude**: one node = one **stage** of this doc's flow; do **not** drill into the internal decisions of a linked sub-doc (that belongs in the sub-doc's own skeleton). In a macro pipeline picture, a "language redirect" is one box; pulling its cookie > header > geo decision into the picture is cross-doc detail creep.
  - **A detail-level flowchart is not allowed**: nodes carrying identifiers or values duplicate the prose at the same granularity, which is a drift source. If a contribution attaches one, extract the facts into prose first (node by node, see `{{ names.verify }}`) and keep only the skeleton.
  - **Plain nodes, no decoration**: no emoji, no `style` / `classDef` lines (pure token noise; same reason as the no-emoji rule above).
  - **Co-locate**: put the picture at the start of the prose paragraph it summarizes, so an editor sees both and does not change only one.
  - Guards: `{{ names.sync_local }}` (AUDIT: a skeleton node with no matching prose stage = structural drift) and `{{ names.verify }}` (the review gate checks the same).
- **Sourcing standard (the most important rule)**: write only **what ten minutes of reading the repo cannot show you**.
  - Write: cross-repo relationships, environments and domains, deployment topology, gotchas, non-obvious design, why it is built this way, who to find when it breaks, "thought it existed but doesn't" facts{{#if has.db_schema}}, and **location pointers** ("which business meaning lives in which table and column"; see §5 "DB location pointers vs data values"){{/if}}.
  - Do not write: which packages are installed, which scripts exist, class or folder lists, anything one `ls` / `grep` shows — an AI entering the repo sees it itself; **changing data values** (concrete row contents, lists of blocked ids, enum value mappings{{#if has.global_settings}}, setting values{{/if}}, id-to-name mappings) — look them up at use-time{{#if has.live_tools}} with the §5 live tools{{/if}}, do not write them in the doc. **Exceptions (two):**
{{#if has.global_settings}}
    - ① A setting value that **is itself the threshold or trigger of a business rule** (for example the `10` in "10 consecutive failures → judged blocked") → may be written once tagged with source, prod and snapshot (§5 business-rule value carve-out).
{{else}}
    - ① Setting-value carve-out: none for this setup — no settings lookup is configured.
{{/if}}
    - ② **Enum / value snapshot — domain docs only.** A table of enum or field values may be recorded as a **complete** snapshot in a **domain doc** when both hold: (a) it is **append-only and long-lived** (existing entries never change meaning, new ones are only appended → zero stale risk) and (b) **without inlining it a standalone reader cannot follow the flow** (someone who installed only this knowledge base, with no repos and no live tools). Record the whole table, never a subset (a subset omits the entry the reader needs). Form: tag `(snapshot YYYY-MM-DD, source <file>)`, state that it is append-only, the code enum remains the live authority, **and always attach a §9 `snapshot tables` anchor** (`source:` + `derive:`) so the audit can regenerate the whole table. **If no anchor can regenerate it, it may not be inlined**: inlining is allowed only because "that one command can regenerate it"; without that it is a hand-kept island and breaks the whole base's auto-freshness. **Project docs do not qualify** — their readers are expected to clone the repo, so inlining is pure redundancy. Test: a domain flow whose standalone reading would break without it → write; stable but greppable and the audience will clone → do not.
  - Large detail (routes, dozens of upstream URLs) **points to source** (for example a router file); do not paste it.
- **"Is it worth writing?"**: ask "would this change a decision the reader is about to make, or prevent a mistake?" Yes → write; just a noun or a list → skip it or point to source.
- **Scope**: {{ scope.rule }} **Pure upstream dependencies** (called but not developed) **get no doc of their own**; list the **project name / API** in the "Dependencies (upstream)" line of your own doc — the name already tells which team to ask; no owner needs recording and no lookup table is needed. What matters is that the "whom do I call" information is there.

