## 1. Language conventions

- **Prose is English.** Draft and finalize in English; there is no translation step.
- **Technical terms stay verbatim**: procedure names, ids, field names, schedules, deployment names, service names, hostnames.
- **`description` in a SKILL.md frontmatter stays English** (trigger metadata).
- **The glossary of a domain doc uses the vocabulary the team actually says** as the canonical terms; do not invent terms.

---

## 2. Where each kind of content goes (layering)

| Content | Where | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| Facts true across the whole platform | reader `SKILL.md` "Platform basics" | time zone, which environments exist, how hosts map to services, where settings live |
{{#if has.observability_doc}}
| Tooling conventions shared across projects | `references/observability.md` | log and dashboard conventions, field meanings, entry points |
{{/if}}
{{#if has.shared_catalogs}}
| Cross-project infrastructure lookups (on demand, not loaded every time) | `references/platform-basics.md` | {{ derived.catalog_headings }} |
{{/if}}
| Values specific to one project | `references/project/<name>.md` | its log identity, ingress hosts, upstream list, platform |
| Build / deploy / conventions shared by one repo family (several siblings) | `references/project/<family>.md` (family overview doc) | the shared build and deploy mechanism of a family of schema repos or micro-frontends |
| An end-to-end business flow across projects | `references/domain/<topic>.md` | a flow touching several projects (detect → decide → enforce); each project links back to `../project/<name>.md` |

- **Judgement**: when the same information would be written again in a second project, lift it to a shared layer; the project doc keeps only its own value plus a pointer to the shared file.
- **Repo families**: when **≥3 siblings** share a mechanism, extract it into `<family>.md` (a family overview doc); **do not park it inside one member** (members are peers, not the authority). A member doc keeps only its own values plus a pointer to the family doc.
- **When a doc should be split into references (default: do not split).** Length is **not** the criterion; the question is "is this doc read whole, or only one section at a time?" A domain doc's value is "the whole flow in one place"; reading it whole is its normal use. Forks come in two kinds; the test is one sentence: **"which branch am I?" → never split; "what does entry number N look like?" → may split.**
  - **Decision forks (never split)**: the reader first has to decide which branch they are on, and the value lies in the **difference** between branches. Splitting them means someone who read branch A never learns that branch B exists with subtly different rules — it manufactures a wrong-branch bug by hand; the counter-intuitive fact §6 asks to be written plainly would be hidden by the file split.
  - **Enumeration forks (may split)**: not a choice between two but a lookup of one entry; each entry is correct without the others.
  - **All three conditions must hold to split**: ① a single branch is ≥1,500 net words (net = minus the anchors block); ② it is an enumeration fork; ③ the hub keeps "how to choose a branch" plus any cross-branch rules, and states both boundaries (use the `domain/index.md` `Covers` notation: `**Boundary**: <what this doc covers> — <what it does not> → <the doc that does>`).
  - Want selective reading without splitting? `grep -n '^## ' <file>` for line numbers and Read with `offset` / `limit` — the same token saving, without the risk a split adds.
- The reader `SKILL.md` is **loaded on every use**: it holds only truly always-on common knowledge. On-demand detail goes in a reference file; `SKILL.md` keeps a one- or two-sentence gist plus a pointer. Test: needed on every task → `SKILL.md`; needed only when a topic comes up → a reference file.
- **The reader `SKILL.md` "How to use" carries a reader-facing live-check contract (item 5)**: it lists the classes of fact where the doc gives *location, not the live answer* (a live incident's root cause; a current config / toggle / setting value; deployment or release status; whether a URL, producer, consumer, job or endpoint is running; a specific case's status; the current contents of an enumeration / blocklist / whitelist; any "is it still the main path" usage claim) and tells the reader to go back to the primary source. It is written for the **consuming reader** (including a PO or PD with only the knowledge base installed, and ops agents) and shares one spirit with the maintainer discipline in §5 / §6 / §9: **never delete this item when rewriting or trimming the root** — it is the only thing stopping a reader from quoting a doc as live truth.

