## 3. Project doc template (the full skeletons are in `{{ names.add_project }}/templates/`)

File name `references/project/<repo-name>.md`.

**Fixed section order (big picture → detail → ops trivia, observability last)**:
What it does → Flows → Architecture → Dependencies → Environments → Data & settings → Caching & retry → Background jobs → Security → Development & testing{{#if has.observability_doc}} → Observability{{/if}}.

- **Always** (a non-deployed artifact has no Dependencies section; its consumers sit under Publishing & usage): What it does, Architecture, Dependencies, Environments, Data & settings, Caching & retry, **Background jobs** (write `none` if there is no scheduler){{#if has.observability_doc}}, Observability{{/if}}.
- **Conditional**: **Development & testing** (omit when the project has no tests); **Flows** (only if the project takes part in a domain flow; §6 bidirectional links); **Security** (only for a notable surface: auth, signing, rate limit, CSRF, CORS, redirect validation; omit for an ordinary service); within Environments, **Health/probes** · **Scaling** · **Deployments** · **Traffic split** (omit when absent or when there is one deployment).
- **Runtime-only sections**: Environments / Data & settings / Caching & retry / Background jobs{{#if has.observability_doc}} / Observability{{/if}} exist only for something that is **deployed and runs**. A pure **non-runtime artifact** (a package library{{#if has.db_projects}}, a DB project{{/if}}) **omits** them entirely (**not** `none` — no runtime means no field, and a leftover `none` is an orphan) and keeps only What it does / Architecture / Flows / the type-specific blocks (Packages / Publishing & usage{{#if has.db_projects}} / Databases / Deploy / Conventions{{/if}}) / Development & testing. A **consumer / worker (has a runtime)** keeps every applicable runtime section.

**Header = a meta list (not a blockquote)**: `# <repo.name>` followed by `- repo:` / `- Owner:` (fixed vocabulary, §6) / `- Audience:` (fixed vocabulary, below) / `- Last updated: YYYY-MM-DD` / `- Verified at: <short-sha> (<commit-date>)`, **plus** a `- Status:` line only when the service is no longer fully operating (after `- Owner:`, see the §6 retired standard). Owner and Audience go in the header meta and **do not get their own section** (no `## Profile` junk drawer). The `Last updated:` string stays so the sync audit can grep it.
- **`Verified at` = the repo commit anchor (project docs only; the drift-diff cursor)**: the commit the doc was actually verified against — `git -C <repo> rev-parse --short HEAD`, with the commit date from `git log -1 --format=%cs` in parentheses. Meaning: "this doc is known to match this commit; `git log <sha>..HEAD` lists the changes not yet reviewed". It divides the work with `Last updated` (the doc edit date): `Last updated` answers "when was this doc touched", `Verified at` answers "which repo commit does it match"; the two may diverge (a typo fix bumps `Last updated` only; re-verifying against a new HEAD bumps both). **A domain doc uses `- Swept at: YYYY-MM-DD` instead** (it spans several repos, so there is no single sha — a plain date cursor): "this doc was fully re-read and re-verified against its involved repos as of this date"; it drives the `{{ names.sync_local }}` domain by-age fast path (upstream quiet and seam anchors complete → only bump the date and skip the full read; past the max age → a forced full read). Its division of work with `Last updated` (content edit date) mirrors the project doc's. **An exception for project docs: `Verified at` may record `n/a (<reason>)`** — a repo mirrored from another source-control system, a family/group overview doc (the anchors sit in the member docs), a repo not cloned locally. **When a doc describes a non-default branch, add `- Tracks branch: <branch>`** (after `Verified at`): `Verified at` then records a sha on that branch and the sync commit range also diffs that branch — without it the default branch's range is empty forever, the cursor advances anyway, and the branch that actually moves is never looked at.
- **`Swept at` = the domain audit cursor (domain docs only)**: a plain date, written only when every repo under `Projects involved` was read in the run. A doc without it was never fully swept, so the AUDIT reads it in full and then adds the date.
- **`Audience` = the exposure / consumer classification (fixed vocabulary; required on every project doc, family overview docs included).** A deployed project picks from {{ lists.audiences_deployed }}{{#if lists.audiences_artifact}}; a non-deployed artifact picks from {{ lists.audiences_artifact }}{{/if}}. Pick by evidence, in this order: ① the Environments `Domains/ingress` (public ingress → the slug meaning public traffic; internal ingress → back-office or internal-service); ② the Purpose wording (back-office / admin UI / ops → back-office). One slug from the list below, no free text; it takes the same value as the Audience column of `project/index.md`:
{{#each scope.audience}}
  - `{{ this.slug }}` — {{ this.meaning }}.
{{/each}}
  - **A single repo that crosses two classes (split by deployment)** joins them with ` + ` (an SDK or package shipped from the repo is not a second deployment); the split mechanism is written under Environments → Deployments, and Audience stops at naming the two classes.

**A banner blockquote is optional; avoid it.** A type or identity (a package library, a non-deployed service…) is written into the `## What it does` Purpose, **not** a heading-less banner (a floating blockquote is an orphan line with no category). A banner is kept only for a clone/sister note, or a critical counter-intuitive fact that belongs to no section. It **must not restate Purpose and must not list which sections were omitted** (the reader can see they are not there).

**Per-section structure rules**:
- **What it does**: Purpose (one sentence) + **Capabilities** (the capability list **does not go in Purpose**; give it its own bullet) + (only with several deployments) Deployments & responsibilities (what each does; deploy mechanics belong in Environments).
- **Architecture**: stack / **runtime version** / layering / style. The runtime version lives **only here**; Development & testing does not repeat it.
- **Dependencies**: upstreams **grouped by domain**, not a comma-separated string; plus Consumed by.
- **Environments**: fixed key:value bullets — `Domains/ingress` · {{#if has.kubernetes}}`Namespace` · `Container/image` · {{/if}}`Health/probes` · `Scaling` (autoscaling + resources) · `Deployments` · `Traffic split` · `Cluster`/platform per env (a table only when it earns multiple columns) · `CI/CD`. **Any multi-fact bullet is split into sub-bullets; no run-ons.**
- **Data & settings**: `Data stores` (**verify the DB engine; do not assume**) · `Cache store` (optional, a peer bullet; a shared-catalog field carries the same store when one covers it){{#each capabilities.shared_catalogs}} · **`{{ this.source_field }}`** (a structured block, see §6){{/each}}{{#if has.global_settings}} · `Settings`{{/if}} · assets / i18n (if present). Each is a **peer bullet** (a catalog field does not nest under Data stores).
- **Background jobs**: `none`, or `scheduler` (framework: storage prod vs non-prod + dashboard path) + `jobs` (name + schedule/trigger).
- **Development & testing**: one bullet per layer — `Unit` / `Integration` / `E2E` (list only what exists).
{{#if has.observability_doc}}
- **Observability** (variants in §5): `endpoints` (metrics, scheduler dashboards) · `logging` (library → sink){{#if has.logs}} · `Server log` ({{ derived.labels.logs }} identity, per-env query){{/if}}.
{{/if}}

**Every label is bold**: `- **Label**:` (a repo-wide convention, applied to every field). **Empty values of a structured field are written `none`** (per-env, catalogs, Background jobs, Caching & retry) so the doc stays uniformly scannable, while an optional bullet or layer (endpoints, a test layer, client log, the Data stores of a stateless project) is omitted instead; but a free-text "not X / nothing found here" explanatory reminder is **not written** — write positive facts only (`uat → none` is a structured value and stays; "NOT the APM name" is an explanatory reminder and goes).

**Parallel or growing lists use a sub-bullet list** (modules, packages, consumers, DBs, jobs, per-env platform…) — never one comma run-on line; adding an item adds a line. A simple two-column value (platform per env, host per env) prefers a list over a table; use a table only when multiple columns need aligning. **Boundary**: items that each carry a description or note (what each library is for, what each module does, what domain each DB serves) → a **list**; a pure list of names (capability names, labels with no description) → one comma line is fine.

- After writing, **always update `references/project/index.md`** with a row (Project | **Owner** | **Audience** | one-line purpose | file). **The main table lists only what is in operation**; retired projects go to the `## Retired` section at the end of the file with different columns (Project | Owner | Retired | What it was / residue | File), see the §6 retired standard. **The Owner column is mandatory** and takes a value from the vocabulary ({{ lists.owners }}), the same as the doc header `- Owner:`. **The Audience column is mandatory**, a slug from the list above (` + ` allowed), the same as the doc header `- Audience:`.
- **Sister / clone project**: open with one sentence "clone of X; most conventions are identical; this doc covers differences only", point common parts back to X, and write only the differences in the body.
- **Repo family (≥3 siblings sharing a mechanism)**: write one `references/project/<family>.md` (a family overview doc) holding the **shared mechanism + a member index table**; each member doc writes only its own values (its DB list / non-prod subset / its own gotchas) plus a banner pointing to the family doc. The shared mechanism is **lifted into the family doc, not parked inside one member** (members are peers, not the authority). This is how "sister/clone" is done when there are many members (the family doc follows the fixed sections itself; a Members section replaces Databases/Components as the index).

**Structure variants** (by **deployment skeleton**, not by frontend/backend or language; the template files correspond):
{{#if has.skeleton_service}}
- **Deployed runtime (`service.md`)**: backend API / frontend / message consumer / scheduler share the full set of sections; differences are marked inline (BACKEND / FRONTEND / CONSUMER / SCHEDULER).
{{#if has.frontend}}
  - **frontend**: Domains records only the **subdomain-prefix pattern** plus a pointer to the domains entry of the reader skill's Platform basics and **does not list every environment's hostname**; the per-env platform is a sub-bullet list; **no Data stores** (a pure frontend has no DB); Caching — a server-rendered frontend splits into `Render server` and `Frontend app`, a static app has only web-server cache + client cache; **Libraries** (the word is Library, not SDK: list the shared modules used){{#if has.global_settings}}; **Settings** come from build-time env or, at runtime, from the backend API, and in the second case the doc still writes how to look the value up, pointing at the namespace that backend reads{{/if}}{{#if has.logs}}; Observability has a browser-side log whose identifier is structured (the identifier plus per-env), or, when errors are only POSTed to the backend, say so in `logging` and omit the client log bullet (do not narrate the investigation){{/if}}; Flows **claims the render chain** of the APIs it calls (§6); health and scaling **must be verified** (a server-rendered frontend has its own health endpoint; a static app's probe is `/`; autoscaling or a disruption budget). A frontend's device gating is usually **client-side**, which is a different path from server-side geo / language redirects.
{{/if}}
  - **consumer / worker**: a deployed runtime → **belongs under service, not artifact**; adds a `<Send/process> chain` block; keeps the applicable runtime sections.
{{/if}}
{{#if has.skeleton_legacy_vm}}
- **VM-deployed app (`legacy-vm.md`)**: Environments = `Deployment` (not containerized) + `Hosts` per env; Observability may be an **APM variant** (a service identity that is not in the project log stream).
{{/if}}
{{#if has.skeleton_artifact}}
- **Non-deployed artifact (`artifact.md`)**: a package library{{#if has.db_projects}} or a **DB project**{{/if}}; **no runtime sections** (Environments / catalogs / Caching / Background jobs / Observability are **omitted, not written as `none`**); keeps the type-specific blocks — library: `Packages` + `Publishing & usage`{{#if has.db_projects}}; DB project: **`Databases` + `Deploy` + `Conventions & gotchas`** (not a single `Scope`){{/if}}.
{{/if}}

