## 6. Hard rules and pitfall cases

**The author capability model (per fact, not per role)** — this kit serves a maintainer (all repos cloned) and an outside contributor (maybe only part cloned, maybe not writing code). **Do not route by role** (never ask "are you an engineer?"); the routing axis is **each fact itself**, decided from the clone inventory the authoring skill probes at the start (`ls {{ paths.repos_root }}`):
- **Code-checkable and the repo is local** → Explore / grep / live tools on the spot, write the plain fact (unmarked).
- **Code-checkable but the repo is not local (still ours, cloneable), or its live check cannot run because the tool is unreachable** → `(unverified — <missing repo or unreachable tool>)` (see "unverified is always marked" below). **Never fill in by guessing and call it verified** — this is what keeps `unmarked = verified` intact; without the probe at the start, a partial-clone author fills in by guessing exactly when verification was due.
- **Authority sits in another team's code/settings that we will never clone** → `(external: <team>)` (see external-owned below). The line between `(unverified)` and `(external:)`: `(unverified)` means "our repo, cloning resolves it, it is a to-do"; `(external:)` means "another team's system, cannot be resolved here, it is the normal state" — marking another team's never-cloned repo `(unverified)` becomes a permanent open item and the semantics are wrong.
- **Not visible in code** → `(human: <{{ git.user_label }}>, <date>)` (see human-sourced below), regardless of whether the author writes code.

"A non-engineer" is just the degenerate case "local repos empty + almost everything human-level"; it needs no separate path. **A contribution is a lead, not a finished fact**: anything from a non-maintainer goes through `{{ names.verify }}` on a machine with the full clone before it is merged — that step resolves `(unverified)`, judges `(human:)`, and sweeps for a whole project that was missed (see "omission" below).

**Marker decision order (go in order; the first hit is the answer)** — the axis is "what must be read to re-verify / is there an artifact", **not** "which team, or whether I personally have access":
1. **Code in a repo you have / can clone** (including another team's repo that we do track) → a code claim, **unmarked**; not cloned yet → `(unverified — <missing repo>)`; a live check whose tool is unreachable → `(unverified — <tool> unreachable)`.
2. **Not code, but a re-readable source** (a data warehouse, a wiki page, DB DDL, a cloud console, a setting store) → a **noncode ref** (a trailing `noncode refs:` + `src:` + `verified:`{{#if has.global_settings}}; a value that is itself a business threshold goes to `global-setting values`{{/if}}). **"I personally lack access" is logistics: write it into `src:` (for example "owned by another team, re-query via them"), not into the marker** — the artifact still exists, re-verifying is still re-reading that source, and colleagues can reach it.
3. **Authority lives in another team's system that we neither clone nor can query** → `(external: <team>)`.
4. **No artifact at all, living only in someone's head** (the why of a decision, who to ask, the reason behind a number) → `(human: <{{ git.user_label }}>, <date>)`.
- One line: **a re-readable artifact → noncode; an artifact in an unreachable external team's system → external; no artifact → human.** {{#if has.owners_external}}Teams in {{ lists.owners_reachable }} are reachable (their repos and data are ours to read), so **they are never external**: access-limited data of a reachable team is a noncode ref, not a human fact. Only teams such as {{ lists.owners_external }} are candidates for `(external:)`.{{else}}Every configured team is reachable, so `(external:)` applies only to a system outside the organization or to a team added later.{{/if}}

**Hard rules**
- **Verify by default; never leave "verifiable but unverified".** Any claim that code, SQL or a live tool can check **must** be actually Explored / grepped / probed before writing, as soon as the **repo is local under `{{ paths.repos_root }}`**; never tag it `(unverified)` to save effort. `(unverified)` (and any "to be verified later") is **only allowed when the repo cannot be found or fetched locally, or the live tool that would check it is unreachable**, and the missing repo or tool must be named. Being able to verify and leaving it unverified is laziness and is not accepted.
- **A concrete literal is always code-checkable; do not close on "reasonable / consistent with some doc / already cross-linked, so probably verified".** A claim that names a **concrete symbol value** (a created-by value, a column, an enum member, a message topic, a status code) is code-checkable and owes a `file:line` **no matter how soft the surrounding sentence reads and no matter that it cites another doc**. **Specificity is the signal: if it can be grepped, grep it.** The close (whether at authoring time or when marking a review claim CONFIRMED) may rest **only** on `file:line` in the owning repo, **not** on "plausible / makes sense", "consistent with the sibling doc", "it is cross-linked so presumably verified", "looks like a domain heuristic, not worth grepping" — those are exactly the reasons a verifiable claim slips through a whole round until someone asks "did you verify this?". A cited or cross-linked knowledge-base doc is **not** a verification source (it can be stale too); the rule "re-verify a migrated or rewritten claim against code, do not inherit it from a sibling kb doc" holds for claims that cite another doc **inline**, not only when folding.
- **Verify the code path that is actually in use; do not trust comments, DDL notes, seed data, history, or dead code.** The authority is the **current code path** — who really reads and writes it — not a `//` comment, a schema description, seed or test data, a `*History*` or legacy table, or disabled code. These go stale like any comment, and **a hit does not mean it is still in use**. When you find a value, ask "is this *running now* or just *lying there*?": after finding the declaration, **trace upward to the active caller** before it counts. A schema note says X but the active service handling the column uses Y → **Y wins and X is marked stale**. When several declarations disagree, the one **called by an active code path** is the truth (→ §0 "sources disagree: converge").
- **"Still alive / still in use / legacy / the main path" is a usage claim and needs a runtime check, not code alone.** Three levels, each stronger: code-present < active-caller (someone calls it) < runtime-live (it actually runs in prod). A claim about a flow's or path's **usage** needs runtime evidence; code alone is not enough. {{#if has.logs}}Check it live against {{ derived.labels.logs }} and carry a §9 `liveness probes` anchor. A sustained 0 is the retirement signal, and zero in a log system does not prove dead (environment-tag quirks, low volume). A usage claim whose log tool is unreachable is tagged `(unverified — <log tool> unreachable)`.{{else}}No log tool is configured for this setup, so a usage claim cannot be written as a code fact: tag it `(human: <{{ git.user_label }}>, <date>)` or record it as a `noncode refs` entry whose `src:` names the runtime evidence.{{/if}}
- **Redact every secret.** Connection-string tokens, API tokens (base64), passwords — record only the DB name / secret name / host / node, **never** the value. **That holds even when the user pastes the secret into the conversation**: connection details go in a secret store; the doc records only the identity of "which machine / which set", no extra connection detail. Say that a credential exists in a source only when that is operationally relevant, never its value and never as filler.
- **A hedge such as "unconfirmed" / "to be checked" is only for guesses not yet verified from code.** A **fact the user states verbally, or a change they clearly say is about to go live, is written as the current state** (a plain statement; no "in progress / not yet / about to" hedging). **What code verified is not separately tagged** (unmarked = verified, §0); a counter-intuitive fact is spelled out in the sentence, with no warning glyph.
- **Human-sourced facts carry `(human: <{{ git.user_label }}>, <YYYY-MM-DD>)`.** An **important fact code cannot show at all** (the business why, ops practice, a historical decision, the reason behind a number, who to ask) is not "not verified yet", it is "**cannot be verified**". Tag it `(human: <the contributor's {{ git.user_label }}>, <today's date>)`. Reasons: ① a code-derived fact can be caught stale by the `{{ names.sync_local }}` AUDIT with a code diff, but a human fact matches no code, so **the audit cannot check it and it would rot silently** — the only way to re-confirm is to ask the person who said it, hence **it must carry person + date**; ② `grep \(human:` lists every manual annotation at once, so deleting, changing and inventorying are easy; ③ a reader (an AI included) knows it is a human assertion, not code-derived. **It is mutually exclusive with code verification**: a fact is either code-derived (unmarked) or human-derived (`(human:)`). An AI reading `(human:)` treats it as "authoritative but possibly stale", **does not correct it with code** (code never had it), but flags it if the code now **contradicts** it. The clone requirement binds code-derived facts only — a code-level fact may not be written without cloning the repo (clone it, or downgrade to `(unverified — <missing repo>)`); human facts **need no clone** (code cannot verify them anyway), and that is the legitimate path for a contributor without repos.
- **Unverified is always marked `(unverified)` (a greppable to-do list).** A claim that **can** be checked with code or a live tool, but whose **repo is not local** or whose tool is unreachable, so it cannot be (see "verify by default" — if the repo is there, always verify) → tag `(unverified — <missing repo or unreachable tool>)` inline. One cause across several environments or lines is one marker on the fact, not one per line, and a fact nobody looked for is not `(unverified)`: look, or leave it out. Later `grep -rn '(unverified' references/` lists every pending item at once. **A source that is not code by nature** (a business rule in a wiki, a network or load-balancer setting, a cloud console, a marketing decision, a workflow tool, an ops SOP) is **not `(unverified)`** — it goes to the trailing anchors' `noncode refs:` (with `src:` + a `verified:` date), which is the A-class list. Do not leave bare prose "not found in code / to check / TODO" scattered around; collapse every one into these two forms (`(unverified)` or `noncode refs:`), or it cannot be grepped later. The one exception is an important gap in a domain doc's `## TODO` (case (b) below).
- **External-owned facts carry `(external: <team>)`.** Authority lives in **another team's** code or settings — we **neither track it** (that repo never appears under `{{ paths.repos_root }}`) **nor can we query it** (no lookup tool we hold reaches their system). None of the three existing states fits: not `(unverified)` (that is "our repo, cloning resolves it, a to-do"; another team's repo can never be cloned, so it would become a permanent open item), not `(human:)` (it has an artifact — their repo or settings tool — it is not asking someone's head), not an ordinary code claim (we cannot Explore or diff it) → so it is its own class. Reasons: ① it is **never audited automatically** by `{{ names.sync_local }}` — when they change code or settings we get zero signal; like `(human:)` it can only land in the weakest layer, "aging + manual", but the cause is the **team boundary**, not code-invisibility; ② `grep \(external:` lists every cross-team contract, so the periodic cross-team review list exists; ③ a reader (an AI included) knows its trust is lower than code-derived facts and checks the other team's system before use. **Pair it with an anchor**: still attach a §9 `noncode refs:` (`src:` naming their repo or tool + a `verified:` date) so the audit reminds by age; the value is still not written in the doc (it changes; look it up when needed). **`<team>`** is an owner code. **Reachable teams are never external** (their repos are tracked, their data reachable by colleagues): access-limited data of a reachable team goes through a noncode ref (see the marker decision order above); do not mislabel it external. **Boundary**: tag only what is truly another team's, unreachable; an upstream we call whose logic we can verify, or simply our own repo not cloned yet, does not count (the latter goes `(unverified)`).
- **Omission is the real residual risk of partial clones (only a full-clone verify catches it).** Per-fact routing solves "I know X is related but did not clone it" (→ still list X and tag `(unverified — <missing repo>)`; verify fills it in). It cannot solve "**I never thought X was also on this flow**" — no claim, no `(unverified)`, no cross-link: it is an **absence**, not a marker, and it escapes the "mark → verify removes" loop. The only fix is the **identifier discovery sweep** that `{{ names.verify }}` runs on the maintainer's full clone (take a doc's message topic / SDK name / endpoint / procedure name / service name and grep every repo, looking for "code that takes part, doc that does not mention it"). `{{ names.sync_local }}` carries the same concern as a quality gate, "`Projects involved` completeness"; mainly a domain-doc matter.
- **A domain doc's `## TODO` is kept in only two cases**: (a) the matching repo is not local or the tool that would verify it is unreachable, and the item carries `(unverified — <repo or tool>)`; (b) it is an **important gap** in that flow, looked at and not traceable, worth flagging, stated as the gap with no marker. Everything else: if the repo is local, verify it into a positive fact; a minor detail you cannot trace and that does not matter is simply stated as what is known, **without a routine "not traced" note** (scattered doubts get read by an AI as authority, and they drag the audit). This is "verify by default" as it applies to domain docs — most repos a flow touches are local, so a TODO that can be cleared is not allowed to stay.
- **Do not assert a prod host from a dev config**; ingress is the external truth.
- **The domain a caller actually hits ≠ the host the ingress lists explicitly**: watch wildcard `server-alias` entries; a caller uses `<sub>.<the current page's domain>`.
- **A counter-intuitive fact ("thought it existed but doesn't", "thought it was the same but isn't") is always written explicitly** — those are the most valuable lines.
- **A retired project or entity always leaves a discoverable record (the retired standard).** A retired project / site / entry **must not just vanish** — residue is rarely cleaned completely, and someone who meets leftovers without knowing what they were is in danger. Four things: ① add a row to the **end-of-file `## Retired` section of `references/project/index.md`** (`Project | Owner | Retired | What it was / residue | File`) — **not kept in the main table** (the main table lists only what is in operation; a retired row in the middle is both noise and gets read as alive at a glance), but in **the same file** and **never deleted**, so searching for "what is this?" still hits it; the File column points at the most relevant surviving doc, and **no empty shell doc is needed for a dead repo**; ② in the most relevant surviving project doc, put a **residue map** table (`Repo | Owner | Location | Verdict`) marking each item **REMOVE** (dead code, safe to clean) or **KEEP** (intentional residue — a redirect, a dormant toggle, a compatibility shim; do not delete by mistake) — **"retired = delete everything" is wrong and breaks live redirects**; ③ a KEEP residue gets a line in the project doc where it lives, pointing back to that RETIRED entry (bidirectional); ④ **a retired project whose repo still exists and whose doc is kept** gets `- Status: retired (YYYY-MM)` in the header and, at the start of `## What it does`, a banner saying "what follows records **how it used to work**, not a live topology". A service where only **part** of the function stopped (for example consumers all marked obsolete and unregistered but the management controller still alive) is **not retired**: add no `- Status:`, keep it in the main table, and say in Purpose which half is dead.
- **There are only two states: retired, or alive**; no middle state. `- Status: retired (YYYY-MM)`, where the month is **when it was actually switched off**, taken from {{ scope.retirement_date_source }}.
  - **A repository host's "last activity" timestamp is neither the retirement date nor the archive date** — any project event moves it (a member leaving, for example). Do not try to infer the retirement date from it.
  - A boolean `archived: true` is reliable for "is it archived", but archiving is often done after the fact and does not equal the retirement moment: it answers "is it dead", not "which day did it die".
  - If you cannot find it, use a known retirement date from a written record (a decommission SOP, say); do not fill in "the month I heard about".
- **No separate `retired/` directory.** ① only a doc or two could go in (a dead repo gets no empty shell doc); ② the AUDIT reads the flat `references/project/*.md`, so a subdirectory would **accidentally** vanish from audit — the direction is right but the cause is a glob that did not reach it, not a rule saying to skip; ③ the path is the identity, and moving a file breaks links pointing at it from chat, wiki pages and old conversations, which cannot be repaired. A header field is greppable for every retired project — as machine-readable as a directory, at zero cost. Revisit once retired docs pile up to eight or ten and the flat layout is really crowded.
- **The AUDIT skip is written in the rule, not left to a glob accident**: `{{ names.sync_local }}` **does not code-reverify** a doc whose header has `- Status: retired` (the repo may still exist and be diffable, but verifying it is meaningless); a repo that is gone is the same.
- **The back-office / internal attribute is carried by the header `- Audience:`; the prose does not repeat it.** Write it once in the header; the body does not add "internal-only" filler.
- **Ownership is decided only by the user, once.** {{#if has.owner_source}}At setup the user named where a project's owner is written: {{ scope.owner_source }}. That source sets the owner, mapped onto the vocabulary; a project the source does not cover is `unknown`.{{else}}No source of ownership was named, so the owner is `unknown` until the user decides, asked in the one question that closes the run.{{/if}} Any other signal (repo path, a different file, a deployment annotation) never sets or auto-corrects the owner (teams reorganize; a deployment tag is a deploy label, not the knowledge ownership definition). The owner is never defaulted to your own team.
- **The header `- Owner:` is a fixed string from the vocabulary; no free text.** Vocabulary (the `project/index.md` Owner column holds the same value): {{ lists.owners }}. Callers go in `Dependencies → Consumed by`, routing in the matching section, and a cross-team "maintained by X" in a separate line — **never stuffed into `- Owner:`**.
- **Do not assume every project has every environment**: list what `{{ derived.first_env }}` … actually exist ({{ lists.env_names }}); read the CI config / deploy manifests for what exists, do not paste a template.{{#if has.excluded_envs}} **Never write a row for {{ lists.env_excluded }}**: an explore pass may report one; filter it out when writing the table.{{/if}}
- **Logging libraries differ per project**; check each project's dependency manifest and registration code instead of assuming.
- **Library two-way update**: when writing a library doc, list the consumers you have verified; conversely, when writing or changing a consumer doc, read its dependency manifest, list which libraries it uses and **which modules it actually uses**. Do not just write "uses X"; write "uses this part of X".
- **Project ↔ domain bidirectional links (mandatory every time, no exceptions)**: every project a domain flow touches is linked **in both directions**: ① the domain doc's `Projects involved` links `../project/<name>.md`; ② that project doc's **`## Flows` section** links back — one bullet per flow, `- **<Flow Title>** — [\`<slug>\`](../domain/<slug>.md)` (`<Flow Title>` is the Topic in `domain/index.md`; flow-specific meaning may be added in parentheses). A project that takes part in no flow **omits the whole `## Flows` section**. **When adding or editing a domain doc, fill in the back-links of every involved project; when adding or editing a project doc, add the back-link of every flow it joins and confirm the domain side lists it.**
{{#if has.frontend}}
- **A frontend claims the flows whose APIs it calls and renders (the render chain).** Whenever a frontend itself calls a flow's API and renders its UI, it claims that flow in `## Flows` **even when the flow's business logic is in the backend**. **Reason**: the value of Flows is "someone who changes a business chain can find every project on it from any end" — a chain strings frontend to backend, and someone changing it must know to touch the frontend; leaving the frontend out because "the logic is in the backend" means the editor cannot find that app. After claiming, add the usual two-way links: the domain doc's `Projects involved` lists the frontend app **as its own entry** (do not fold it into the backend's "their apps" summary). Frontend docs in the old format may list too few flows; add them when converting.
{{/if}}
- **A service with several deployments**: do not just list the names; write **each one's responsibility** + the split key (env var / reverse-proxy conditional routing) + why it is split.
{{#each capabilities.shared_catalogs}}
- **The `{{ this.source_field }}` block**: every project doc of a **runtime** project (service / consumer / worker) carries a `- {{ this.source_field }}:` line (a pure library / DB project has no runtime → omit it entirely, do not write `none`) — either `none`, or the {{ this.name }} it uses with its mechanism, followed by one sub-item per environment (a host, or `in secret (<name>)`). Which cluster each environment uses affects troubleshooting, so say it. The full roll-up is `references/platform-basics.md` ("{{ this.heading }}").
{{/each}}
- **Do not write speculative bugs**: a DI / config branch chosen by environment is a fact and may be written; "this is a prod bug" is a guess and is omitted.
{{#if has.external_reference}}
- **An external reference ({{ derived.labels.external_reference }}) can be out of date: reference, not authority.** A link may be kept as a reference, but **code is the single source of truth**: whatever can be checked in code is decided by code; the wording is "reference (may be stale)", never "maintained there / source of truth". **Exception**: a list that code mixes across projects and cannot separate (an asset list, say) is still decided by the external reference.
{{/if}}
- **Check the full set of ops fields for every service; do not assume they are absent because of its type**: Health/probes, Scaling (autoscaling + resources), Background jobs, metrics endpoint, Security — go into the repo for each; if present write it structured, an empty per-env, catalog, jobs or caching field is `none`, an optional bullet the repo lacks (Health/probes, Scaling, endpoints) is omitted, and so is a whole absent section. **A VM-deployed or older app may have them too** (an older API can have a scheduler self-monitor plus token validation); do not assume "older ones lack these".
- **Verify the nature; do not be fooled by a string**: a `HealthCheck*` symbol may be an internal scheduler self-monitor, not an inbound probe; a metrics-looking name may be a substring coincidence. After a grep hit, confirm the semantics (inbound vs outbound, a real metric vs a coincidence) before writing.
{{#if has.database}}
- **Verify the DB engine**: read the data-access registration and the dependency manifest; **do not infer from "the package exists"** (a project can reference two engines' packages while its identity store actually runs on a third).
{{/if}}
{{#if has.db_schema}}
- **DB: record locations, not values**: a location pointer (which business meaning lives in which table and column) may be recorded — a mapping code cannot show and that is stable, anchored to the schema repo (§5); **do not record changing data values** (row contents, blocked-id lists, enum value mappings{{#if has.global_settings}}, setting values{{/if}}) — look them up live. The test: "where to look" goes in the doc, "what was found" does not. A whole-table dump points to source; keep only the columns that answer the current question.{{#if has.global_settings}} **Exception**: a setting value that is itself a business-rule threshold → §5 business-rule value carve-out.{{/if}}
{{/if}}
{{#if has.external_reference}}
- **The per-env public host comes from the external reference ({{ derived.labels.external_reference }})** plus repo verification; the naming convention (see the reader `SKILL.md`) is only a fallback — it has exceptions (a short alias that does not follow the prefix); do not force it.
{{/if}}

**Pitfall cases (real cases; read them as counter-examples).** The table lives in `{{ names.local_rules }}.md` under "Pitfall cases (§6)" so that a regeneration never overwrites it. Record one row per real mistake: **Wrote** (what was written) / **Truth** (what was actually true) / **Lesson** (the rule it taught). A lesson that generalizes beyond this knowledge base belongs in the hard rules above, not only in the table.

