## 7. Consistency conventions

- Header = **a meta list**: `- repo: <url>` / `- Owner: <vocabulary>` / `- Audience: <vocabulary>` / `- Last updated: YYYY-MM-DD` / `- Verified at: <short-sha> (<commit-date>)`, plus `- Status:` only when retired (no blockquote; `Verified at` is for project docs only, with `n/a (<reason>)` as the exception, see §3). **A domain doc uses `- Swept at: YYYY-MM-DD`** (a plain date cursor in place of `Verified at`, see §3). A domain flow's back-links go in the `## Flows` section (§6), not in the header.
- Section titles (English, fixed order in §3): What it does / Flows / Architecture / Dependencies / Environments / Data & settings / Caching & retry / Background jobs / Security / Development & testing{{#if has.observability_doc}} / Observability{{/if}}.
- Header `- Owner:` names only the owner (a fixed vocabulary value, §6) — no caller, consumer or routing notes.
- A counter-intuitive fact / gotcha is written into the statement (no warning glyph); a fact code cannot show uses `(human: <{{ git.user_label }}>, <YYYY-MM-DD>)` (§6){{#if has.observability_file}}; how to query logs is always "see `observability.md`"{{/if}}.
{{#if has.db_schema}}
- A DB column location uses one consistent path notation, with an anchor `repo: <schema repo>`; locations only, never data values (§5).
{{/if}}
- Style follows §0: impersonal statements, concise, no emphasis glyphs or conversational asides.
- A source difference is annotated in one fixed form: "config says `X`, but the value reaching the log system is `Y`".
- Large detail points to source (a router file, say); do not paste it.
- **Domain doc structure:**
  - **Header = a meta list**: `- Last updated: YYYY-MM-DD` + `- Swept at: YYYY-MM-DD` (the domain audit cursor, the analogue of the project `Verified at`; a plain date with no sha, left out until a sweep has read every involved repo, see §3){{#if has.external_reference}} + an optional `- External reference ({{ derived.labels.external_reference }}): <id or url> — reference, may be stale`{{/if}}. **No `- Owner:`** (a flow spans projects), no env/platform table, no log identity (those belong to project docs).
  - **Sections come from the standard vocabulary, in a fixed order, only when they apply** (omit an empty one; no empty shell): Background / why → **`## Flows`** → named sub-mechanics (`##`, for example detection sources / status model / money flow / page gating) → Projects involved → Key rules / constraints → Glossary → TODO (the anchors come last).
  - **`## Flows` is the mother section**: each end-to-end flow is one `### Flow A — <name>` (a single-flow doc still uses one `### Flow`); it parallels the project doc's `## Flows`. Each step names a concrete mechanism plus a project link (steps are code-verified and not separately tagged; a step code cannot show carries `(human: …)`).
    - **Appearing in a chain ≠ being the authority — write the roles when they differ (for incident work).** A component being in a flow does not make it the authority for that concern. When **the caller ≠ the decision authority**, **the producer ≠ the final writer**, **the config owner ≠ the runtime decider**, or **the topology ≠ the live root cause**, write the role **into that step's statement** (for example "the enrollment API is only the caller; activation is decided and written by the progress consumers"). This is the commonest trap in an incident: knowing which components exist but not which one is the root cause. **Do not add a separate authority-matrix table**: role differences are facts of the flow's own steps — write them in prose and hang them on the existing §9 seam anchor; forcing a cross-concern authority table is hand-kept and invites unverified guesses.
  - **Every project reference is a markdown link** `[\`name\`](../project/<name>.md)`, never a bare path `→ ../project/x.md`.
  - Inside a section the writing is the same as in project docs (§3): one fact per bullet, bold labels, parallel items as lists, large detail points to source, impersonal and concise.
  - **Every project listed under Projects involved needs a back-link bullet in that project doc's `## Flows` section (§6 bidirectional links).**

