## 9. The anchors block (machine-readable references that speed up AUDIT; a domain doc carries one, a project doc when it is cross-repo or multi-deployment or holds a claim that needs an anchor class)

A doc may end with an HTML-comment `<!-- anchors -->` block (invisible to readers and renderers) that lets the `{{ names.sync_local }}` AUDIT locate things fast. A project doc carries one when a claim's evidence lives in another repo (a shared contract, an SDK, a sibling deployment) when the project has more than one deployment, or when it holds a claim that needs a class (an external or non-code source, a setting value that is a rule's threshold, a usage claim); its block lists only those claims, while a domain doc always carries one covering every code-checkable claim. Leave out an anchor class that has no entries.

- **Use symbols, not line numbers**: `file:line` rots with every commit; anchors record **greppable symbols** (class / method / procedure / toggle / message topic / middleware / channel id) — they move only when the code does.
- Format:
  ```
  <!-- anchors (machine-readable for {{ names.sync_local }} AUDIT; not rendered)
  code claims — grep pattern in repo; present=ok, missing/renamed=drift:
  - <claim> | repo: <repo[,repo]> | grep: <pat1|pat2>
  noncode refs — re-check source manually by age:
  - <claim> | src: <external page / workflow / schema DDL / not-traced> | verified: YYYY-MM-DD
{{#if has.global_settings}}
  global-setting values — re-verify via {{ capabilities.global_settings.lookup }}, prod, by age:
  - <claim/value> | website: <settings namespace> | key: <Key> | env: prod | verified: YYYY-MM-DD
{{/if}}
  snapshot tables — re-derive from source file on AUDIT (an inlined value table; §0 carve-out ②):
  - <table name> | source: <repo/path/to/file> | derive: <how to regenerate rows, e.g. enum members → `Value | Name`>
{{#if has.logs}}
  liveness probes — re-run the log/metric query on AUDIT; sustained 0 over the window = retirement-review flag (NOT auto-delete):
  - <flow/path> | index: <log stream> | query: <identity + phrase match, or metric> | expect: >0 over <window> | verified: YYYY-MM-DD
{{/if}}
  -->
  ```
- **Existence ≠ correct behavior**: a grep hit only means the symbol is still there, not that the logic is unchanged. Anchors are "fast location + rename/removal detection", **not a substitute for trust** — the authoritative drift judgement is still the `Verified at` (project doc) / `Swept at` (domain doc) cursor plus the repo diff (§3 / `{{ names.sync_local }}`); when the diff shows a substantive change in an anchored area, read that area.
- **Coverage is the audit's labor-saving lever**: the sync fine filter uses anchors as a relevance probe (`git log -G'<pat>' <range>` tells whether this range touched a claim). **So in a doc that carries an anchors block, every code-checkable claim should be reachable by an anchor** — an unanchored claim can only be caught by the periodic full re-read backstop, which is pure cost. The more complete the coverage, the cheaper and safer every later audit.
- **A domain doc must anchor the cross-project seams**: a flow's truth lives *between* repos — message topics and schemas, RPC definitions, shared libraries, setting triggers, routing rules. These are where an upstream change silently bends the flow while the doc's own symbols do not move, so they **must have an anchor** (pointing at the matching upstream repo) for the AUDIT to trigger re-verification when a shared or contract repo changes. The anchors cover **every repo in `Projects involved`**, not just the main one.
- A non-code ref writes no grep; it writes `src` + a `verified:` date so the AUDIT picks the longest-unverified for manual checking by age (a workflow tool, an external page, DB DDL never appear in a code diff). **`(external: <team>)` facts take this class too** (§6 external-owned): `src:` names their repo or tool; the AUDIT can only remind by age and cannot diff automatically.
{{#if has.global_settings}}
- **A global-setting value (§5 business-rule value carve-out)** takes the `global-setting values` class and **writes no grep** (it is not in code; it is a runtime value): record `website` + `key` + `env: prod` + a `verified:` date. The AUDIT picks by `verified:` age and re-reads the setting — only a value that *is* a business threshold or trigger belongs in this class; a pure ops setting is not recorded (back to the §5/§6 general rule: look it up). **A value still being there ≠ the mechanism still being alive**: if the claim asserts a mechanism ("a pool", "a gate", "a toggle in effect"), the probe must, besides verifying the value, grep the **reader** of that key in the owning repo — if no code reads it, however right the value is, it is an orphaned setting and the claim is drift. (A measured case: a "backup-domain pools" claim held when probed in the morning, and only later did it turn out that no code had read those keys for months.)
{{/if}}
- **An inline snapshot table (§0 carve-out ②)** takes the `snapshot tables` class: record `source:` (the code file that produces the table) + `derive:` (how to regenerate the rows from it). This is what keeps an inline copy from becoming a hand-kept island — when the `{{ names.sync_local }}` AUDIT meets such an anchor it **re-reads the source file and regenerates the whole table** (not a grep for existence: an append-only enum gaining one member still matches a symbol grep and the omission goes unseen). **One iron rule**: a value table with no `snapshot tables` anchor that the AUDIT can regenerate **may not be inlined** — derive on demand only. Inlining is justified only by "that one command can regenerate it". **The shared-layer roll-up variant**: a cross-project roll-up catalog (a table of every project's value of one field, grouped) also takes this class, but its `source:` is **the docs it rolls up** (plus any underlying manifest), not a single code file, and `derive:` states how to rebuild it from those docs. `{{ names.sync_local }}` **reconciles and regenerates** it after the project sweep (the source facts are already in hand, near-zero cost), so the roll-up stays fresh and is not a hand-kept island — the one exception to "the shared layer is hand-kept and outside per-claim audit" (only a re-derivable roll-up qualifies; the reader `SKILL.md` and the shared references stay hand-kept).
{{#if has.logs}}
- **A liveness probe (runtime-live ≠ code-present)** takes the `liveness probes` class, for **usage** claims — "this flow / path is alive / in use / the main path / retired": code being complete does not mean it still runs. Record one query that should return `>0`, plus the `expect` window and a `verified:` date. The AUDIT re-runs it: **a sustained 0 = a retirement-review flag, not an automatic deletion** — zero in a log system does not prove dead (environment-tag quirks, low volume, prod logging to a non-prod stream all produce false zeros). Orchestrator-only (agents cannot reach the live tools).
{{/if}}
- **By-age cadence (when to re-verify)**: a runtime anchor (`noncode refs`{{#if has.global_settings}} / `global-setting values`{{/if}}{{#if has.logs}} / `liveness probes`{{/if}}) **need not run on every audit**; it ages by its own `verified:` date: `noncode refs` **{{ audit.by_age_days.noncode_refs }}d**{{#if has.global_settings}}, `global-setting values` **{{ audit.by_age_days.global_setting_values }}d**{{/if}}{{#if has.logs}}, `liveness probes` **{{ audit.by_age_days.liveness_probes }}d**{{/if}} (or the fact's natural change cycle). Not yet expired = skip this time (the `{{ names.sync_local }}` manifest triage computes the work list from it instead of blind-scanning every runtime value); past the **max-age backstop (suggested 2× the cycle)** it is forcibly re-verified whether or not upstream looks quiet. A code anchor goes by commit-range pickaxe (verified when touched) and does not use this date axis.
- **Forced full re-read, at two levels: per doc ({{ audit.domain_swept_max_age_days }} days) and per run ({{ audit.full_sweep_max_age_days }} days)** (this is the axis for "is the doc read at all"; it is a **different axis** from the runtime-anchor cadence above, which is untouched):
  - **Per doc (domain)**: `Swept at` older than **{{ audit.domain_swept_max_age_days }}d** → that doc is force-read in full however quiet upstream is or however complete its anchors are; the fast path is void.
  - **Per run (whole sweep)**: more than **{{ audit.full_sweep_max_age_days }}d** since the last **full** sweep → this run goes straight to full mode (the full-sync override in `{{ names.sync_local }}`).
    A project doc has no field saying "when was it last read whole" (`Verified at` advances on every pass, fast-pathed ones included, so it is always fresh and cannot serve as a backstop); the run-level full mode fills the hole more cheaply than a new field. **The one skip that remains is the project-doc empty-commit-range** — git proving 0 commits in the range is a fact, not a guess.
  - **A doc that carries an anchors block not covering its claims has no backstop at all** — it is fully re-read every run until coverage is complete.
- Large detail (a full procedure table, a long SQL block, 50 error codes) still **points to source**; anchors only record "where to grep / where to re-verify".
- **Grep matches file contents, not file names**: to verify that a file exists, pick a stable string **inside** the file; do not use the file name as the pattern — `grep -r` does not search file names.
- **Pilot mode = run once after writing, and run again before the AUDIT starts (gate 0)**: grep every code anchor and check it hits. Verifying after writing is so you do not write a broken one; **re-verifying before the AUDIT is because the audit itself uses anchors to locate code — a broken anchor makes a whole run treat false MISSes as drift and hides a real rename behind constant noise**. Measured on a base of about 1,700 anchors: checking whole-anchor hits found 24 broken ones in the first round, and **the second round, checking per alternative with `-E` / `-F` side by side, dug out 21 more plus 6 dead always-matching alternatives** — all of which had already been used as locating tools for who knows how long. Gate 0 is purely mechanical, zero judgement, a few minutes; if it fails, **fix the anchors first, and only then go on**.
- **An `A|B|C` alternation has to be checked alternative by alternative, not as a whole.** Any alternative hitting lets the whole anchor pass, so a dead one can carry a wrong fact silently (measured: an anchor's second alternative named an **inverted** class and rode through on a live first alternative for a long time, and the doc body went wrong with it).
- **Attribute a MISS first; two piles, two treatments — mixing them manufactures false failures:**
  - **A broken pattern → must fix** (change the pattern, not the claim and not the repo): ① the pattern is not a legal ERE (`grep -qE <pat> /dev/null` returns rc=2); the commonest cause is an **unclosed `(`** copied in from an inline call (`startServer(8080`, `retryAsync(3`); ② **legal but never matching** — `(` `)` read as a group and `{ }` as an interval, so `Config("X")` finds nothing though the line is right there (only **actually running it** shows this; checking legality does not); ③ a pattern that starts with `--` is read by grep as an option; ④ a **file path or file name** used as a pattern (see above: grep matches content, not names); ⑤ one half of a multi-repo anchor can never hit in that repo — **but first tell which kind**: a symbol that lives in only one repo (shrink the repo list and add a separate anchor for the other side's real symbol), or an anchor that is **a division of labour** with each repo holding one half (`ClientQuery|ClientConfig|CREATE TABLE \[dbo\]\.\[Client\]` with three alternatives split between a service repo and a schema repo). The latter is **correct**, and shrinking the list would delete a real claim; ⑥ **legal, hits, but hits something else** — an unescaped `[...]` becomes a character class, so `CREATE TABLE [dbo].[Domain]` matches `CREATE TABLE dbo.Config`. This one is more dangerous than ②: the target can be wiped out entirely and it still goes green, and **hit counts alone can never reveal it**; ⑦ the pattern includes the **closing `)` of an inline call**, but real code has further arguments (`StartsWith("T_", StringComparison...)`, `Map("/ping", async`). Escaping does not save this; **cut the trailing parenthesis**, leaving the text up to the first argument.
  - **A correct MISS → never change it**: the `Class.Method` composite (each side exists but the combination is not a literal string), and an **inverted claim** where the doc says "deleted / orphaned / retired" (the absence *is* the claim). Inverted ones must be marked on the line, otherwise the next round will "fix" them as broken patterns.
    - **"Marked" has only two machine-checkable forms, and the classifier recognizes only these two**: (a) the **uppercase token `INVERTED`** on that anchor line (in the claim label, or in a comment separated from the pattern by two spaces); (b) the line sits under an `INVERTED anchors —` sub-block header (to `-->`; that sub-block must be the last part of the anchors block). Other wordings — `a HIT means …`, `(expect MISS)`, `a hit is not proof` — **do not count**, and the classifier may not guess from reading English. Measured: judging by meaning went wrong in both directions — missing lines in the sub-block and lines that only said "a HIT means", reporting them as failures; and reading "a hit is not proof" inside a positive anchor's label as inverted, then deleting a genuinely absent claim, which a verifier overturned. A legacy line with only `(expect MISS)` has a **missing label**: add `INVERTED`, and leave the pattern alone.
  - One line: **"the code is clearly there but the pattern finds nothing" = a broken pattern; "the code is really not there" = the MISS is correct.** If you cannot tell, go read that code; do not guess from how the pattern looks.
  - **The unit of verification differs from the unit of reporting; do not mix them.** The rule above has you verify **alternative by alternative** (otherwise the dead one hides behind a live one); but results are reported **per whole anchor**, because the anchor's contract is "at least one of these hits", not "every combination hits" (see ⑤, the division-of-labour shape). Reporting per (alternative, repo) pair makes every repo of a division-of-labour anchor emit its own false MISS: a measured run reported **138** of them this way, and after converging to whole anchors only **2** remained — 136 were phantoms. And those 2 mean one fixed thing: **an anchor missing in every repo and every alternative can only be two things — real drift, or an inverted anchor with no `INVERTED` label.** Read the label first, then the code; add the missing label rather than rewriting the pattern.
  - **To separate the two piles mechanically, run the same alternative with `-E` and with `-F`**: `-E` 0 hits and `-F` hits = the literal is there and the regex ate it = ②/⑥/⑦; both 0 only then go read the code. The reverse does not hold: `-F` 0 hits does not mean broken — a pattern may deliberately use `.*` or already be escaped.
- **An always-matching alternative stops the whole anchor from ever detecting drift**: alternation passes if any alternative hits, so `LicenceController|public async Task.*Get` stays green even when the real symbol is gone completely (measured: 6 anchors of the shapes `public async Task.*Get` / `.*bool` / `class.*Validator`). The test: **if this alternative would match in any repo of the same language, it is dead weight**; cut it and keep only the real symbol, or narrow it to a form that carries a real symbol (`AddScoped.*Validator` → `AddScoped.*IComplianceRuleValidator`).
- **Separate the annotation from the pattern with two spaces**: `grep: X  (INVERTED — expect MISS)`. With only one space the parser takes the annotation into the pattern (measured: 6 anchors).

