## 10. Audit / review discipline shared by `{{ names.sync_local }}` (whole-kb audit) and `{{ names.verify }}` (changeset review)

Both skills check a doc's claims against code and live tools one by one. The shared **discipline** lives here; each SKILL.md keeps only its own **scoping mechanism** (sync-local: manifest triage / pickaxe / the phased pipeline / waves; verify: changeset scoping / the overlap-dedup gate / the report format). For every principle below the skills **point here and do not restate it**.

- **The completeness contract = build the per-claim work list first.** Before verifying anything, list every claim in a table (sync-local = the audit **manifest**; verify = the claim **inventory**) and classify each. **The definition of done**: every row either carries a `file:line` verdict from the owning repo (verified / DRIFT / WRONG / NOT-FOUND) or sits in the **irreducible-open** set (`(human:)` / `(external:)` / a runtime value §0 says not to record). **There is no intermediate state, and no row may be dropped silently.** The test: if the user can later ask "did you verify everything?" and you can find one claim that was not checked, the contract was not kept — and that follow-up question is exactly the symptom this contract exists to eliminate. Give the work list to the user once at the start (the full scope plus the finish line); do not trickle it.
- **Internal consistency of a doc is a second axis with its own cursor and needs its own pass.** `Verified at` / `Swept at` track **whether upstream moved**; "one doc contradicting itself in two places" is produced by **editing the doc**, and has nothing to do with upstream. So the empty-commit-range skip is right on the code-drift axis and a **blind spot** on this one — measured: after a whole-base audit reported "complete", a read-through of every doc turned up 26 self-contradictions (a summary row that did not follow the body, a Projects-involved line still carrying the framework the body had just overturned, a glossary treating a coincidence the body called "just a coincidence" as a rule, an anchor and the prose spelling the same symbol two ways).
  - **Scope = the docs this run edited, a closed set the run itself knows** — no new header field is needed. The reasoning: a doc nobody edited cannot grow a new self-contradiction; only edited ones can.
  - **It has to be a separate closing pass, not something done while writing**: catching a contradiction in a paragraph you wrote ten minutes ago is inherently harder than doing it with fresh eyes a round later — this is the only real cost of this axis and the reason it needs its own pass.
  - **What to read: the whole file**, not the diff. The other half of a contradiction is nearly always in the half you did **not** touch, and **the highest-frequency half is the labels in the anchors block**: numbers and versions in a label evolve separately from the body, and the body changed while the label did not (measured: 5 of the 15 contradictions in one run had this shape — `3 deployments` against a body saying 4, `~105 jobs` against a body saying "100/92, stop approximating", an SDK version against the build file, autoscaling written for only one of the deployments). After that come the summary rows, `Projects involved`, `Key rules`, the glossary. A diff cannot show them.
  - **Sync the anchor label at the moment you change the body, not at the closing pass** — the closing pass will catch it, but how many it catches tells you how many you missed earlier: in one measured run the closing pass found 13 self-contradictions and **7 had the same shape**, the body corrected and the same doc's anchor label still claiming the old thing. So the rule lives **in the editing action**. **This does not conflict with "do not do it while writing" below; they govern different actions**: that one governs **detecting** a contradiction you just wrote and cannot see (it needs distance); this one governs **propagating** a correction you already know to the copies you already know of (mechanical, needs no distance, and delaying it only makes the volume grow). After changing a claim, grep that fact in the **same doc** (the symbol name, number, version, mechanism name, the "has / has not" word) and change every copy before moving to the next claim (where copies live: see "sweep the same batch of tokens in two directions" below, direction ②; the closing pass is a batch sweep before commit, this one is at editing time). The closing pass's job then becomes picking up the remainder, not the bulk.
    - **A grep pattern still going green does not mean the label is not wrong** — the sneakiest kind: an anchor labelled "prod autoscaling differs between the two deployments: one 10–15", while the body had changed to both being 5–10, and the pattern read `minReplicas: 5|minReplicas: 10` — both literals still in the manifest, so it **always passes**. **The direction-② token sweep structurally cannot catch it** — the tokens are still there so the scan passes; what is wrong is the label's semantics. Only reading catches it. (The same run had three of the same shape: "two workloads" left in a Purpose sentence, a route total left in two places, a count left in a third place.)
  - **Numbers a doc writes itself are counted on the spot**: "N validators / RPCs / controllers / methods" sits next to its list, and the two not matching is the second most frequent shape of this kind (6 of 15 in one run — 3 against a 4-row table, 13 against 14 RPCs, 13 against 16 controllers, 21 against 22 packages…). Reading cannot catch this; you must count — **and first confirm your counting method matches the doc's** (a measured near-miss: counting with a wider pattern almost turned a correct 92 into a wrong 96).
  - **The existing backlog is counted separately**: docs edited in earlier runs that never got this pass are standing debt; queue them oldest `Last updated` first, and do not mix them into this run's closed set pretending they were done together.
- **Claim classification (four classes)**: **code-checkable** (naming a concrete literal — a column / enum / created-by value / message topic / status code — makes it one, however soft the surrounding sentence reads and even when it cites another doc; if it can be grepped it owes a `file:line`, see §6 "a concrete literal") / **human-sourced** `(human:)` / **external-owned** `(external:)` / **convention** (markers, cross-links, header meta, anchors, retired rows). The marker decision order is in §6.
- **Verify to the level the claim asserts (the §6 three levels)**: code-present < behavior-traced < runtime-live. A usage assertion (legacy / main path / unused / still active) is a **runtime** property; code cannot answer it, so it must be checked live. Do it in one go; do not conclude from the cheapest level.
- **Resolve, do not defer — never hand a verifiable claim back to the user.** Whatever code or live tools can reach is verified in this pass; the user decides only `(external:)` items and preferences. Parking a verifiable claim as "waiting for the user to confirm" guarantees one more round trip and is the number-one cause of audits that "never finish and find new unverified things each round". **A correction you just made (a relabel, a fold, "now covered") is itself a new claim** — it is not verified because you wrote it; before committing, re-run it against code and tools and actively try to falsify it. **"Re-run" is mechanical, not the few things you remember**: extract **every** code identifier in backticks from this run's diff (CamelCase types / methods, dotted namespaces, procedure names, message topics, ports, schedules) and grep **all** of them back in the owning repo; every MISS from the batch scan is re-verified one by one, and after that every remaining claim is read back in the sentence it sits in.
  - **Sweep the same batch of tokens in two directions; missing either loses a whole class.** ① back to the **owning repo** — is this identifier still in the code (the existing rule); ② back to **the whole `references/` base** — find the **other copies** of the same claim. A rule almost never lives in one place: the summary-table row, the `Projects involved` bullet, `Key rules`, the glossary, a cross-reference in another doc, and the anchor's own name. If you changed the canonical copy and the copies still say the old version, you get "every doc reads right alone, and following the old one gives the wrong answer". Measured, this class accounted for about a quarter of what the read-through caught (a counter renamed in the body only, its old label left in Projects-involved; a transport written two ways in two docs; a framework the body had overturned still living in four anchor names). Direction ② costs one grep; missing it costs the next whole audit. Sampling misses whole classes of error: in one measured run, a sweep wrote 1,010 lines, ~40 sampled claims were read and called "clean", and a mechanical scan then found 8 real errors — 4 wrong names (copying them would not compile) and 4 where every identifier was right and **the surrounding sentence dropped a conditional branch**. The latter cannot be scanned for and only reading catches it, but the mechanical scan shrinks what must be read to an amount that can be read in full.
- **A correction that makes a claim smaller must carry "what I searched to conclude it does not exist" — because grep is worst at proving absence.** The wariness §10 shows toward an agent's negative claims applies equally to **your own corrections**, with heavier consequences: writing a wrong narrowing note ("exists only in tests", "no caller", "dead", "retired", "nobody reads this setting") **tells the next reader to stop looking**, which is worse than no note — measured: a "FALSE-PASS FIXED: the symbol exists only in unit tests, not in production code" note even led to narrowing the anchor's grep, while the symbol was in fact a property of a production model and a real transform value in config; the two places in the body were right, and it was this note that overturned the right ones.
  - The rule: whenever a correction **narrows** a claim's scope, write the **basis for the judgement** in the doc or anchor (which repos were searched, which patterns, which call sites were read), not only the conclusion. The next round can then re-verify it, and see at a glance where the first search missed.
  - **The three searches most often skipped**: **directories other than tests** (models / config / runtime settings / deploy manifests, not only the service-layer source); **config values count as production use** (a transform or cluster id in a runtime config file is a live mechanism); **search every repo, one by one** (one repo having none does not mean all have none).
- **§9's "existence ≠ correct behavior" as it looks in self-verification: "the mechanism is right, the consequence is missed" is the main way things slip through.** §9 says an anchor hit does not mean the logic is unchanged; the same thing in self-verification is — every identifier is right, but **the surrounding sentence omits a conditional branch**. The three costliest measured cases all had this shape: on a non-prod environment a pre-filter and a `.Single()` condition were mutually exclusive so that API always threw; a global query filter made the same query return different rows in different environments; a repo name was left out so the reader grepped in the wrong repo and found nothing. Scanning cannot catch these; only reading can. **A proxy probe** shrinks what must be read to something readable (cheap, high hit rate, **not exhaustive**): for each repo the doc names, grep for constructs where "the same code takes a different path per environment" — environment checks, global query filters, test-mode flags; this codebase's current forms are whatever the stack uses, and the list is not exhaustive: add a new form when you meet one — then, after excluding startup / registration noise, compare against whether the doc recorded each branch. Anchor self-verification must split alternatives one by one (§9 pilot mode).
- **Close = terminate, do not trickle**: the closing report is exactly three parts — ① **done** (what this pass verified / fixed), ② **irreducible-open** (the `(external:)` / `(human:)` / an important gap kept in a domain doc's `## TODO` / preference rows in the manifest or inventory that structurally cannot be verified here — **no user action needed unless they want to override**), ③ **one next action** (usually push). **No "anything else to verify?" menu.**
- **Agent output is a lead, not a verdict — adversarially re-verify every one.** A fan-out agent has not loaded this skill and is a blank Explore agent; it is unreliable on (a) cross-file judgements (which config is prod), (b) cross-doc claims (it did not read the other doc), (c) **negative** claims ("no autoscaling / no Flows" — mostly under-grepped), (d) **anything settled on the first grep hit** (a TTL overridden by a decorator, a value also set elsewhere, an exact count, a wrong repo). Measured, about 30% of "simple-looking" findings are wrong, and the wrong ones are exactly the "simple" ones, so risk-tiering does not work. The pipeline is **find → a second pass that traces the full call graph (every set-site / decorator / override / fallback, matching the doc text verbatim) to confirm → only then propose / apply**; never find → apply. Plausibility, "consistent with the sibling doc", "it is cross-linked so presumably verified", "looks like a domain heuristic not worth a grep" are **not verdicts** (§6); a cited knowledge-base doc is **not** a verification source (it can be stale).
- **Who runs the second pass, and when, matters as much as whether it runs — leave these two undefined and find → apply sneaks back in through the side door:**
  - **Who**: the agent that found it **cannot verify it itself**. The same agent re-reading its own claim only re-runs the same wrong grep and confirms itself. The second pass goes to the orchestrator (mechanical checks) **plus another agent**, and the brief must say "go and **refute** it", not "go and confirm it".
  - **When**: verify **before the unit that produced it closes** — a wave, a changeset, a batch. Do not accumulate it to the end of the whole run, when the context is fullest and most likely already compacted, and the evidence behind the claim is exactly what a compaction eats. The same total work, in the right place, catches it; at the end it becomes "found only when someone asks later".
  - **Revert what is overturned on the spot**, while the context that produced it is still there. Reverting three waves later costs re-reading all of it again.
- **Omission sweep — absence is not a marker, and only a full clone catches it**: a per-fact marker solves "I know X is related but did not clone it" (→ list X, tag `(unverified — <missing repo>)`); it cannot solve "**I never thought X was also on this flow**" — no claim, no `(unverified)`, no cross-link (§6 omission). The only fix: take a doc's identifiers (message topic / SDK or client name / RPC service / endpoint / procedure name) and `grep -rl` every clone under `{{ paths.repos_root }}`, looking for "code that handles the identifier but is not in the doc's `Projects involved`" → **MISSING-PROJECT** (resolve the directory with the repo-dir map in `{{ names.sync_local }}` step 1, "Map to repo(s)"). **The bar: that project is a live step of the flow the doc describes, and holds on the origin default branch (or `Tracks branch`).** None of these count: only sharing the same DB, only a package reference with no call, hits only in tests / docs / tool caches, a caller only on an unmerged branch. (Measured: without this bar a domain finder reported every one of those as a missing project, and the verifier had to invent a standard on the spot.) A partial-clone contributor structurally cannot run this (a repo they do not have cannot be grepped), so it is the **exclusive** duty of `{{ names.verify }}` (its step 3b) on a maintainer's full clone; `{{ names.sync_local }}` treats the completeness of a domain doc's `Projects involved` as a quality gate.
- **A scan proves itself first, and only then do you conclude from its result**: before trusting any grep / sweep, run **one must-hit and one must-miss** control. A scan with no control that returns 0 hits cannot tell "really none" from "my tool is broken", and the latter is the norm — measured: one scan reported 1,402 MISSes, all of them bugs in itself. **A batch-scan MISS is a hypothesis, not a conclusion**: re-verify one by one before touching a doc (the reverse is not needed: a hit is a hit). **The controls are the rule; the list below is only the pits already stepped in, not the whole set of pits** — having checked the list does not excuse you from running the control; new tools, new flags and new versions all produce ones not listed. Measured pits (a ripgrep case): **no `-E`** (that is `--encoding`; the pattern gets read as an encoding name and the whole scan fails), **hidden files skipped by default** (CI and deploy anchors need `--hidden` or every one gives a false MISS), `-o` with a multi-pattern file **reports only one of overlapping matches**. Two that need no flag: `--no-messages` plus `2>/dev/null` swallows the real error and reads silence as "no match"; truncating with `head` / `cut` and concluding from the first half (measured: this once caused a wrong statement to the user, and again later when `head -5` almost concluded "the client never calls that endpoint"). Shell layer (zsh): `"$R:path"` has its `:h` / `:c`… eaten as variable modifiers, so always write `"${R}:path"`; zsh does not word-split `$g`, so a multi-word `for g in $list` loop should run under `bash -c`; an unquoted `--include=*.md` is read as a glob and fails with `no matches found`; and when `grep` is aliased to ugrep, a long `.{0,90}` context regex fails with `exceeds complexity limits` — that is an error, not 0 hits.
- **A control proves "the tool works", not "the coverage is right" — that is a different failure and needs another control.** The must-hit / must-miss pair can both pass while the pattern only covers a small slice of what was supposed to be scanned, and the returned results **look like a complete scan**. Measured: a repo-wide scan for one package's version declarations required the version to follow the package name immediately, and missed every declaration written with a backtick between them; another caught only the full constructor form and missed every abbreviated call — **only 5 of 34 docs were scanned, and both times the gap was found only because an agent happened to report it**. So before any repo-wide scan take one more step: first count the population with the **loosest** pattern (just the name), then count again with the pattern you will really use; **a large gap between the two numbers means the coverage is missing**, not "it just happens that only a few docs have it".
- **Owner / secret / log rules** are the same as §6 / §5: the owner follows §6{{#if has.owner_source}} (decided by the user once, as the source they named){{/if}}, and any other signal only flags, redact every secret{{#if has.logs}}, verify log values with a live query and do not trust config{{/if}} — shared by both skills, not restated by each.
